  "notes": "OG Parker's frequent partner, and the research separates them on exactly one axis: Deko is the more chordal of the two, with EDM-style suspensions and added 9ths. That is what this model encodes and what keeps it from being a copy of its partner's file — `susSubstitutionProb` 0.4 against the genre's 0.15 (the highest here), `ninth` at 0.3, an `open` voicing spanning 50–84, and `melody.densityPerBar` [5,9] for the moderate-to-high density the paragraph names, with the chord bed plus arpeggio expressed as `countermelody.styles` led by `arp` at `densityRatio` 0.6. The dance habits also change the shape of the record rather than only its chords: the intro is a fixed 8 bars with a build, so `drums.build.riserBars` is authored and `switchUpProb` is raised to 0.25 for the riser/filter re-entries. The 808 is sometimes doubled by a filtered synth bass an octave up, which is why `bassline.register` reaches 35 — the widest span in this batch after ATL Jacob. ⚠ `medium`, and the research is explicit about why: it rates the *duo's* method medium-high but his individual habits only medium. The inferred parameters are all of the drum numbers — the paragraph gives him \"bouncy kick\" and \"triplet hats with rolls\" and nothing more — and the i–iv–VI–VII family at weight 2, which is INFERRED.",
